[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  thursday june 30th i'm julie chang for  the wall street journal filling in for  zoe thomas  celsius one of the largest crypto  lenders has been under watchful eyes  from investors in recent weeks in  mid-june it said it was freezing all  withdrawals swaps and transfers between  accounts for its more than one and a  half million users citing extreme market  conditions  and last week it hired restructuring  attorneys for a potential bankruptcy  filing  celsius had pitched itself as less risky  than a bank and even said it could offer  better returns but according to investor  documents from 2021 reviewed by the wall  street journal the lender had little  cushion in the event of a downturn or  mass withdrawals  as of last summer it had 19 billion  dollars in assets and about 1 billion  dollars in equity by comparison north  american banks and the s p 1500  composite index typically have a median  assets to equity ratio of less than half  that according to data from factset  here to tell us more about the latest  insights into celsius's business is our  reporter elliott brown hi elliott thanks  for coming on the show thanks for having  me  so elliot can you start us off by  telling us about how celsius its ceo or  the company pitched itself to the crypto  market celsius was  one of the sort of higher yield crypto  platforms out there so what that means  is they were basically saying anyone who  has crypto you can sort of store your  money with us like a bank and then we'll  give you really high interest rates and  the reason that we can give you these  high interest rates is because we're  passing along all the profits that we  get from business that we do and we're  giving them to you whereas a bank just  keeps your profits and and gives them to  fat cat executives so that was  essentially the pitch okay so that's how  celsius presented itself to potential  customers but you've been looking at  investor documents what did you find  yeah so we got a hold of essentially  what they were offering to investors in  the company itself which is different  from sort of depositors who gave crypto  to celsius and those basically paint a  picture that celsius was this really  high risk highly leveraged bank with  very little cushion uh in case something  went wrong and those are sort of the  recipes for all the reasons that banks  have regulations because if a bank is  going to be storing your money and  giving you interest  if you want to withdraw your money then  it's really important that the bank can  do that and of course what's happened  with celsius is you know in mid-june  they froze withdrawals uh because they  were unable to give people the money  that they wanted back so people gave  them money and then they couldn't get it  back and so that's the uh essential  problem that they have presumably  because uh some of these risks played a  role though we don't have the specifics  on that and what has celsius said about  all of this  celsius has been relatively mum they  basically said that you know in mid-june  they said that they were freezing  withdrawals because of extreme market  conditions and  otherwise they didn't comment to us for  the story and have stopped really  communicating and the journal has  reported that they've hired advisors to  advise on a potential bankruptcy filing  so we know things aren't going great  over there  you mentioned that celsius didn't have a  lot of money sloshing around but it had  a cushion as you've said it was holding  on to a lot of money from crypto  investors so  where did all of that go  so basically celsius did have a business  model akin to a bank which is when a  bank takes your money  and you deposit it that bank is usually  taking that money and it doesn't just  keep it in a vault ready to hand you  back bags of cash it goes and then gives  people small business loans or gives  people mortgages  so  what celsius was doing is whereas a bank  might have relatively stable loans that  it's doing to make money for most of its  its funding celsius was lending to a  whole bunch of other financial firms in  the crypto sector and so crypto is  considered just this incredibly high  risk product or area to lend and that's  where like some huge portion of  celsius's  you know deposits were going to  invest or lend to other things in in  crypto land and that also included they  made their own invested in their own  bitcoin mining operation and so if you  deposited money with them then they're  taking that money and essentially buying  like servers to mine bitcoin which is  kind of hard to then immediately give  you your money back if you want it back  the next day  do you have a sense for when things  started to go wrong for celsius was  there one moment that was just like the  turning point for it  with them we don't because it was a  pretty binary thing it was uh well from  what we've seen publicly it was pretty  binary it's there there wasn't enough  money they weren't able to fulfill  enough requests to take money out then  they said we're freezing withdrawals uh  now generally in crypto land what's been  happening is  in the past number of months the  declining value of all cryptocurrencies  has led to  you know so far it seems to be this kind  of slow one domino to the next uh maybe  that's not the best analogy but clearly  there's some connection when bitcoin  goes down by 60 70  then people who had investments in that  and those investments were sort of tied  to other investments there gets to be  this effect that you know spreads from  one to the other so does that affect  what's referred to as contagion in the  crypto sector yeah so i think the big  fear right now is much like we saw in  the 2008  financial crisis  when  lehman failed and and then we all  thought that you know merrill lynch  could fail and and you know one bank  failing was going to lead to another  because one bank lent to the other and  other people then you know their loans  went to zero and then nobody had enough  money kind of riding around and the  government had to step in  um i think that's a fear of what could  happen here alright that was our  reporter elliot brown elliot thanks  again for being here thanks for having  me  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts i'm julie chang for  the wall street journal thanks for  listening  [Music]  you

